C = [
("h1", "6. The Commission and Its Confirmation"),
("p", "Claim: the Lord sent the Twelve to Israel with power over sickness and devils, and the passages that report the sending state that the word was confirmed by signs. The writer of Hebrews describes that confirmation in the past tense, to readers who had heard it second-hand. The volume reads three passages, Matthew 10 with Luke 9, Mark 16 and Hebrews 2, in the setting each gives itself."),
("sub", "6.1 The Twelve sent to the lost sheep"),
("p", "Matthew records the sending in plain terms: “These twelve Jesus sent forth, and commanded them, saying, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. And as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand. Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give” (Matthew 10:5–8). The commission has an audience, “the lost sheep of the house of Israel”; a message, “The kingdom of heaven is at hand”; and four acts, healing the sick, cleansing lepers, raising the dead and casting out devils. The acts are commanded in the same breath as the message."),
("p", "Luke reports the same sending: “Then he called his twelve disciples together, and gave them power and authority over all devils, and to cure diseases. And he sent them to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick” (Luke 9:1, 2). Power is “given,” and the sending is for two things, to preach the kingdom and to heal the sick. In neither Gospel is the power described as a gift for the apostles’ private use. It is given for the work of the mission."),
("p", "The passages state the limit of the sending as plainly as its content. The Twelve are not to go to the Gentiles or enter a Samaritan city. The Twelve in the Gospels are men on a mission to Israel with the Kingdom message and with signs that accompany it."),
("sub", "6.2 Mark 16 as written"),
("p", "Mark records a longer commission after the resurrection: “Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature. He that believeth and is baptized shall be saved; but he that believeth not shall be damned. And these signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils; they shall speak with new tongues; They shall take up serpents; and if they drink any deadly thing, it shall not hurt them; they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover” (Mark 16:15–18). The passage lists signs, names those they follow, “them that believe,” and ties them to the preaching: “And they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following. Amen” (Mark 16:20)."),
("p", "The textual question that attends Mark 16:9–20 belongs to Study 25, and the volume leaves it there. The volume reads the passage as it stands in the KJV and puts three questions to it. The commission is spoken to the eleven (Mark 16:14, 15). The signs follow “them that believe,” and the closing verse reports who went forth and what followed: “they went forth, and preached every where, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following.” And the verse is in the past tense. It records what happened when the eleven went out."),
("p", "The passage joins preaching and signs and states their relation: the word is confirmed by the signs. The volume reads the confirmation as the same confirmation the earlier chapters traced, a messenger’s word attested by an act of God before the people he addresses. The commission is to the eleven, and the people they address in the Gospel and in Acts are Israel and those who meet Israel’s messengers."),
("sub", "6.3 The seventy and the order of Luke 10"),
("p", "Luke records a second sending: “After these things the Lord appointed other seventy also, and sent them two and two before his face into every city and place, whither he himself would come” (Luke 10:1). The seventy return with a report: “Lord, even the devils are subject unto us through thy name” (Luke 10:17). The Lord answers: “Behold, I give unto you power to tread on serpents and scorpions, and over all the power of the enemy: and nothing shall by any means hurt you. Notwithstanding in this rejoice not, that the spirits are subject unto you; but rather rejoice, because your names are written in heaven” (Luke 10:19, 20)."),
("p", "The seventy go before the Lord’s face to cities where He is about to come, and the power given them is for that mission. The Lord’s last sentence ranks the power below something else: the names written in heaven. The passage states the order, and the volume reads it as written. The signs served the mission, and the mission served the Lord’s coming to the city."),
("sub", "6.4 Hebrews 2: the word confirmed, and in what tense"),
("p", "The letter to the Hebrews describes the same confirmation in the past tense. “Therefore we ought to give the more earnest heed to the things which we have heard, lest at any time we should let them slip. For if the word spoken by angels was stedfast, and every transgression and disobedience received a just recompence of reward; How shall we escape, if we neglect so great salvation; which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord, and was confirmed unto us by them that heard him; God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will?” (Hebrews 2:1–4)."),
("p", "The passage has a structure. The salvation “at the first began to be spoken by the Lord.” It “was confirmed unto us by them that heard him.” God bore witness to those who heard Him “with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will.” The verbs “began,” “was confirmed” and “bearing them witness” are past in relation to the readers, and the readers are those to whom the confirmation came second-hand, through “them that heard him.” The readers are Hebrews, and the argument of the letter runs on the covenant and priesthood of Israel. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting."),
("p", "The passage does not tell the readers to expect signs. It tells them to heed the word, because the word was confirmed. The signs are the ground of the warning, “How shall we escape, if we neglect so great salvation.” The writer names “gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will” as a part of that witness and does not list them. The volume carries the passage no further than the letter carries it, and it does not carry it to the Body of Christ."),
("ctbl", "The commission and its confirmation.", [
    ("Passage", "Audience", "What the text states about the signs"),
    ("Matthew 10:5–8", "The Twelve, sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel", "Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils"),
    ("Luke 9:1, 2", "The Twelve", "Power and authority over devils and diseases, given for the mission"),
    ("Luke 10:1, 17–20", "The seventy, sent before His face", "Power over the enemy; the names written in heaven are set above it"),
    ("Mark 16:15–20", "The eleven (16:14)", "Signs follow them that believe; the word was confirmed with signs following"),
    ("Hebrews 2:1–4", "The Hebrew readers, who heard it second-hand", "The word was confirmed by those who heard Him; God bore witness with signs, wonders, miracles and gifts"),
  ], [0.22, 0.34, 0.44]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The Twelve are sent to Israel with a message and with power over sickness, devils and death.",
    "The seventy are sent to the cities where the Lord is about to come, and the Lord sets the names written in heaven above their power.",
    "Mark’s commission is spoken to the eleven, and its last verse records the confirmation in the past tense.",
    "Hebrews 2:3, 4 describes the confirmation in the past tense to readers who received it from those who heard the Lord.",
    "None of the three passages is addressed to the Body of Christ, and none is carried to it here.",
    "The textual question of Mark 16:9–20 is left to Study 25.",
  ]),
]
